  "announcement": "Direction 02-panel-column announces the next run best. It provides a stable, narrow vertical list in the instrument-panel style where each routine displays 'next Mon 06:00' (or 'in 23 min') in quiet cyan mono type, persistently visible in peripheral vision without requiring any click or hover. Unlike the drawer (hidden) or the page (navigated away), it stays on the Mission Control screen. Unlike the inbox, it does not mimic the visual weight of a human message or an amber alert, so it whispers rather than shouts. Unlike the schedule tape, it requires no graphical parsing of timelines—just reading down a list. It treats an on-time run as the normal, calm state (background text) while reserving amber only for held or failed runs, perfectly matching the requirement that normalcy whispers and exception speaks.",
  "acts": "Placement in 01-ghost-card or 02-panel-column reads best as acts. When Skip once and Run now appear as small, cyan-outlined text buttons (or ghost buttons with thin borders) at the far right of the routine row or ghost card, they read as momentary interventions—'do this once now'—rather than persistent state toggles. Their placement inline with the rhythm text ('every Monday 06:00') reinforces that they are exceptions to the rule, not replacements for it. This leaves conceptual room for the chat to propose permanent rhythm changes ('change to Tuesday') as configuration proposals requiring discussion and approval, keeping the UI surface clean: the button is for immediate agency, the chat is for governance. Toggles imply 'this is the new state'; these buttons imply 'I am overriding the clock once,' which is the correct mental model forSkip once and Run now.",
